Theorem IC-T4: Contract-boundary composition over the CFG ledger

  Preconditions:
    - The T2 CFG-ledger model is the substrate: per-partition-class event
      ledgers (BIRTH/CREDIT +1, CONSUME -1, READ floor 1, MUTATE floor
      1 + live-siblings) derived from instruction-shaped input by the
      computed classification through the committed RL-2 twelve-kind
      terminal-use table; the three-clause per-class per-path invariant;
      the clauses-safety equivalence.
    - The boundary carrier: a per-param contract whose access field is the
      COMMITTED AccessClass (the RL-32/RL-34 Owned/Borrowed verdict) plus
      three fields the committed corpus lacks, defined as the T4 carrier -
      iter-consumes (grounds the RL-2 ApplyToIterConsumingParam transfer
      kind), transfers-through-return (grounds the RL-34 transferOwnership
      tail-call action and the RL-2 iter-consume-Return overlap), and
      sharing-view-producer (a read-only co-owner view result). A grounding
      constructor reads the access dimension straight off the IC-2/IC-3
      ParamContract.
    - The call-site expansion is COMPUTED from the contract, never free
      input: the argument's terminal-use kind is selected by the contract
      fields into the committed RL-2 vocabulary (Owned -> ApplyToOwnedParam;
      Borrowed + iter-consumes && !transfers-through-return ->
      ApplyToIterConsumingParam; Borrowed otherwise ->
      ApplyToBorrowedParam), and a transfers-through-return or sharing-view
      contract appends the result-binding dup that credits the source class
      (the returned/view reference re-entering the caller's scope; ret is
      tier-1-unified with arg - the same allocation flows back / the view
      shares it).
    - Invoke successor selection occurs after the argument boundary event. The
      same computed argument event therefore prefixes BOTH normal and unwind
      paths. Only successful normal return binds the result and may append the
      passthrough/sharing-view credit.
    - Indirect-call explicit operands use one caller-borrowed logical contract.
      Each backend projects that contract into its own calling convention. The caller
      therefore contributes READ for every explicit operand and retains its
      ownership obligation. The closed target identity supplies frozen
      per-parameter ownership facts projected exactly from the final
      IC-2/IC-3/IC-8a ParamContract. The normalized demand is Borrow,
      WholeValue, or ProjectedField(f): Owned, borrowed iter-consume without
      return transfer, and borrowed COW-consume demand WholeValue; an exact
      projected iter-consume demands only that field; plain Borrow demands no
      owner. WholeValue and ProjectedField together are contradictory. A
      WholeValue/ProjectedField fact requires exactly one logical adapter
      CREDIT before target entry, which the target discharges on every normal
      and unwind exit; Borrow requires neither.
    - Target-parameter facts carry stable (function, parameter) identity; result
      facts carry stable (function, normal-return-site) identity. Every related
      result must name the same function and parameter. EntryCredit additionally
      requires exact demand/relation topology: WholeValue may transfer only to
      Direct/Contained, and ProjectedField(f) only to the same field f. This
      topology rule is source-sensitive: a TargetFunded result may legitimately
      reshape after consuming the entry owner while preserving function and
      parameter identity. Independent results require no synthetic entry fact,
      so zero-parameter functions may return a fresh owned value; related
      results require an entry fact.
    - The verified CFG supplies one authoritative exit inventory. Normal exits
      carry their computed ownership requirement; unwind exits carry no result
      requirement. Function-plan freezing derives the canonical normal-site
      list from that inventory, rejects duplicate exit/proof identities, and
      requires exact one-for-one evidence coverage. Owned rows are recomputed by
      the per-site source freezer and compared with the claimed fact, preventing
      a correct-key forged source. Ownerless rows carry a nonzero stable proof
      identity, semantic reason, and proposition tying the proof to the
      CFG-owned ownerless requirement. No result evidence constructor exists for
      unwind. These are logical ledger obligations, not selections of a counter,
      object layout, retain opcode, ABI layout, or discharge mechanism.
    - The callee side consumes the SAME committed table lookup: the callee's
      per-param-class ledger opens with the transferred-in reference (a
      construct of the param - BIRTH) exactly when the boundary use kind
      transfers ownership in; a borrowed contract opens nothing and is
      checked under the borrow assumption (entry count 1 - the caller's
      retained live reference) with net zero.
    - The compiled Lean model is the calculus authority; conformance of the
      shipped emitter to the model is established empirically on the
      compiler's ARC-verified test surface, not discharged here.

  Soundness property:
    The contract classifies the boundary event for each argument's
    partition class per the fixed table - owned param (callee side) =
    BIRTH; owned arg (caller side) = CONSUME; iter_consumes &&
    !transfers_through_return = CONSUME; borrowed = READ;
    transfers_through_return = PASSTHROUGH, modeled explicitly as
    CONSUME-at-call + CREDIT-at-return netting zero; sharing-view producer
    = CREDIT on the source class - and composing per-function three-clause-
    satisfying ledgers through those contract-classified boundary events
    preserves ledger safety end-to-end: a caller whose ledger satisfies the
    three clauses WITH the summary in place, and a callee whose OWN ledger
    conforms under the contract-mandated opening, yield a three-clause-
    satisfying (hence balanced-and-safe) inlined ledger - caller prefix,
    callee body events, result binding, caller suffix. The contract IS the
    interface: the callee is consumed only through its conformance, never
    re-derived. A MISCLASSIFIED boundary is provably rejected: an owned arg
    classified borrowed leaves the caller a release obligation the callee's
    real free doubles - the composed ledger nets -1 (clause 1 false, the
    double-release shape). The live-at-call side condition (running count
    >= 1 at a transfer-in handoff) is honest and NECESSARY: a CONSUME
    summary carries no floor, and dropping the condition admits a composed
    use-after-free. On Invoke, omitting an Owned argument consume from the
    unwind path is rejected; treating result credit as common to unwind is also
    rejected because no result exists there. For indirect calls, the exact
    adapter rows are caller-Borrowed/target-WholeValue =
    [READ, CREDIT, CONSUME], caller-Borrowed/target-ProjectedField(f) =
    [READ, CREDIT_f, CONSUME_f], and caller-Borrowed/target-Borrow = [READ] on
    both exits. All preserve the caller's incoming obligation. Missing the
    owner-demand credit consumes the caller's obligation; missing its unwind
    discharge leaks the adapter credit. The CFG-total result plan proves every
    normal site exactly once: an explicit Ownerless row adds net zero, while an
    Owned row adds exactly one owner sourced by an independent target birth, an
    exact-topology entry-credit transfer, target funding, or a normal-only
    result credit. No row exists on unwind, no owned source receives double
    credit, and an entry-derived result has exactly one unwind discharge.

  Proof obligation:
    (P1) Classification grounding. Each table row is a COMPUTED fact: the
         Owned contract selects ApplyToOwnedParam and derives [consume];
         the iter-consume-no-return contract selects
         ApplyToIterConsumingParam and derives [consume] (the committed
         RL-2 iter-consume transfer - the callee's iterator machinery is
         the release); the plain Borrowed contract selects
         ApplyToBorrowedParam and derives [read]; the Owned
         transfers-through-return contract derives [consume, credit]
         netting zero (the explicitly-chosen passthrough form); the
         sharing-view contract's result binding derives [credit] on the
         source class (the view result minting the credit fact); the
         boundary expansion never places a release (the RL-34
         no-post-tail-call-dec law at the boundary level), and the Owned
         boundary computes transfer-in through the SAME committed RL-2
         verdict that RL-34's transferOwnership action reflects. The
         callee-side owned-param row: the callee ledger opens with BIRTH
         exactly when the boundary use kind transfers ownership in.
         Invoke path row: argument event precedes both exits; Owned consumes on
         both; result credit is normal-only. The negative witness proves an
         empty Owned-unwind event stream contradicts the boundary model.
    (P2) The interface-complement extraction. A conforming callee's body
         ledger nets EXACTLY what the caller-side summary event nets
         (transfer-in: -1, the handed-off obligation discharged inside;
         borrowed: 0, nothing released) and its floor clauses hold from
         entry count 1 - pinned by the callee's OWN three clauses under the
         contract-mandated opening (threeClauses (birth :: B) forces
         net B = -1 and floors of B from 1; the borrow-assumption form
         forces net B = 0 and floors from 1). Floor monotonicity carries
         the entry-count-1 floors to the caller's actual call-site count.
    (P3) THE composition theorem. The caller's summary-composed ledger
         splits at the call site (the committed mutate-free derivation
         split + the committed RL-2 bridge); substituting the callee's body
         events for the summary event preserves clause 1 by net additivity
         (net B = net S) and clauses 2/3 by the floor decomposition (B's
         floors from the call-site count via monotonicity + the live-at-
         call side condition; the suffix re-enters at the SAME count since
         the nets agree). The operational corollary converts through the
         committed clauses-safety equivalence: the composed inlined ledger
         is balanced-and-safe - no leak, no use-after-free, no COW
         corruption. Per class, per path; the callee never re-derived.
    (P4) Negative witnesses. The MISCLASSIFIED BOUNDARY is rejected on the
         computed witness (identity classes over the real empty-edge
         union-find): the callee conforms to its TRUE Owned contract and
         provably does NOT conform to the Borrowed misclassification; the
         caller's summary-composed ledger under the misclassification
         passes in isolation, yet the inlined composition nets -1 (clause 1
         false; operational count -1 - the double release); the correct
         classification composes safely end-to-end. The LIVE-AT-CALL
         necessity witness: a caller stream handing off at count 0 passes
         its own clauses (CONSUME has no floor) and the callee conforms,
         yet the inlined composition reads at count 0 - the operational
         machine observes the use-after-free; the side condition cannot be
         dropped.
    (P5) Borrowed indirect-adapter composition. The frozen target fact is the
         exact inward-owner demand derived once from the full final parameter
         contract: Owned, borrowed iter-consume without return transfer, and
         borrowed COW-consume demand one whole-value owner; projected-field
         iter-consume demands exactly that field; plain Borrow demands none.
         Whole + projected is contradictory and fails closed. The adapter
         contributes one CREDIT iff the frozen demand is WholeValue or
         ProjectedField. A target body is consumed only through the existing
         calleeConforms interface: owner-demand body net -1 is paired with the
         adapter credit; Borrow body net 0 receives no credit. In either case
         the composed local segment nets zero and its floors hold from the
         caller-borrowed entry count 1. Separate normal and unwind target
         conformance hypotheses establish both paths. IC-8a's CONSERVATIVE seed
         freezes WholeValue, so non-enumerable closure targets necessarily use
         the [READ, CREDIT, CONSUME] row rather than guessing ownership from a
         physical representation.
    (P6) Function-total result provenance and callable composition. Result facts
         are keyed by (function, normal-return-site); entry facts by (function,
         parameter). Independent ownership requires no entry and an explicit
         complete target birth. Every related relation requires the same entry
         function/parameter. EntryCredit additionally requires exact topology:
         WholeValue -> Direct/Contained, ProjectedField(f) -> the same field f;
         mismatched fields and Direct/Projected cross-shapes fail closed.
         TargetFunded reshaping remains admitted when its complete root/payload
         certificate is valid. The CFG exit inventory computes the canonical
         normal requirements. Duplicate identities, missing/extra/duplicate
         rows, cross-function/site replay, Owned/Ownerless category swaps,
         zero/wrong ownerless proof identities, wrong reasons, forged owned
         sources, and evidence for unwind all fail closed. A universal theorem
         ties every accepted plan back to that exact CFG inventory and proves
         row-count coverage. Appending an Ownerless row to the P5 zero-net
         segment preserves net zero; appending an Owned row yields net +1 and
         preserves floors. Both append nothing on unwind. EntryCredit is
         discharged exactly once there; target birth/funding and normal-only
         result credit mint nothing there.

